		<published>2006-12-31T06:47:00+00:00</published>
		<id>https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=3052#p3052</id>
		<link href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=3052#p3052"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Adding Joint Makes Collison Spongy (Sequential Impulses)]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=3052#p3052"><![CDATA[
One limitation of most iterative methods is that they aren't stable when you have a low mass body (wheels) supporting a higher mass body (chassis). This can be resolved by:<br>1) Using more iterations<br>2) Faking the masses (make the wheels heavier than they would normally be).<br>3) Using rays instead of wheels (my preferred solution). This requires a custom suspension+friction contact constraint.<br><br>Also, make sure your code has joints and contacts in the same iteration loop. This helps them <em class="text-italics">see</em> each other.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=12">Erin Catto</a> — Sun Dec 31, 2006 6:47 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
